Principal stresses, rotated axes and invariants (@mech-stress-transformation: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "Elastic-constitutive-laws-and-the-assumptions-they-carry.lingua#r_7PD9HSJTERFS77Q3TRSX6CAVP1") { r_2AVTQVMWSHVEAER99D61KQZNM8
**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Tensor Transformations; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), sections 2.3 and 9.2, with the tensor appendices.

Changing coordinate axes changes tensor components while leaving the physical traction relation intact. With an orthogonal coordinate transformation Q, use σ′ = QσQᵀ consistently with the chosen vector convention. Principal stresses are eigenvalues of the symmetric tensor; traction is normal to a principal plane.

For a planar stress block, the two in-plane principal stresses are the mean normal stress plus or minus √(((σ_x−σ_y)/2)² + τ_xy²). In the example σ_x = 100, σ_y = 20 and τ_xy = 30 MPa, they are 110 and 10 MPa. Include the third principal stress, zero for plane stress, when evaluating three-dimensional maximum shear: it is (110−0)/2 = 55 MPa, not merely the in-plane circle radius 50 MPa.

Trace, determinant and principal values are unchanged by an orthogonal axis change. Hydrostatic stress is the mean principal stress times the identity; subtract it to obtain deviatoric stress. A von Mises equivalent value measures a particular deviatoric invariant, not a new stress acting on a surface.

**Work:** Rotate the example by several angles and recover the same principal values and traction on a fixed physical plane. Construct Mohr's circle from the equations with declared shear conventions. Check a hydrostatic stress, pure shear and uniaxial tension before using equivalent-stress criteria.

**Return:** [Mechanical engineering and machine design](../Mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design.lingua#r_0017YZ63HZBZ4P4WD8DECVWE4R).
